═══ R8 EN reply complete (collagen/vegetarian) — guard: clean ═══
Q: I have spondylosis and osteoporosis and my doctor requires me to take collagen as part of treatment. But I have been a vegetarian for 14 years. Can I take normal collagen?
Sources: Metaphysics Q&A Vol 3 (EN) | Buddhism Q&A (EN) | Metaphysics Q&A Vol 3 (EN) · types: qa_metaphysics,qa_general,introduction
  ✓ substantial reply (>600 chars)
  ✓ ends cleanly (punctuation/emoji, not mid-word)
  ✓ answers the vegetarian-collagen question
  ✓ final text passes guard (0 residual)
--- reply ---
Thank you for asking instead of just deciding on your own — that care itself shows how seriously you take your vow 🙏

**Master Lu addressed this exact situation** in *Answered — Frequently Asked Questions About Practising Buddhism* (Q116 area, on vegetarian precepts):

> "When you consume any medicine or health supplements that contain ingredients such as gelatine or fish oil, if your body does not reject the taste, then perhaps you have not reached the level where you are completely observing your precepts. However, it would still be best to avoid consuming such products as they contain animal by-products."

So the direction is clear: **avoid animal-derived collagen if there is any way around it.**

**What I'd suggest, in order:**

**1. Ask your doctor for a non-animal option first.**
Please tell your doctor plainly: "I've been vegetarian 14 years, are there plant-based or non-animal alternatives?" For bone and joint support there are usually options — vitamin D, calcium, vitamin K2, silica, plant protein and amino acid supplements that support the body's own collagen production. Many doctors will work with you if you ask. Master Lu never asked us to neglect our health:

> "We should ensure that we have a balanced diet, as we need to take care of our bodies. Buddhas and Bodhisattvas would also like us to have a healthy body so that we can practise Buddhism better."

**2. If, after checking, there is genuinely no alternative and your health requires it** — Master Lu taught that under circumstances where you truly have no choice, you say in your heart to Guan Yin Bodhisattva that you are taking it because you have no choice, and you do not want to at all. Keep the intention clean: it is medicine, not food, and there is no enjoyment in it. Then continue your vegetarian practice in everything else.

The key, as Master Lu emphasised, is **intention** — a supplement taken under medical necessity with a reluctant heart is not the same as eating meat because it tastes good.

**3. Alongside the medicine, use the Dharma.**
Master Lu taught that bone and physical conditions also carry karmic causes. The Great Compassion Mantra for health and strength, and the Eighty-Eight Buddhas Great Repentance to repent and eliminate the karmic obstacles behind the illness, are the tools he gave us. Heart Dharma Door is body-and-mind together — keep following your doctor, and let the chanting work on the spiritual side.

May I ask — **are you already doing daily recitation?** If yes, I'll suggest what to add for your bones. If not, I'll give you a simple starting point 🙏

If you'd like to speak to someone locally about supplements and precepts, the volunteers are free to help:
📞 +603-6257 3811 | 🌐 https://xlfm.my/contact-us

